repository-code: "https://github.com/GregoryMorse/SelfModTamper"
abstract: >-
  Self-modifying code (SMC) creates unique challenges when it comes to timing
  semantics on modern hardware for accurate emulation by a maximally efficient
  non-SMC equivalent. We investigate the efficacy of using polymorphic SMC to
  produce a tamper-proofing mechanism that utilises high-accuracy timing
  measurements which cannot be reproduced by translating the code from the von
  Neumann architecture to a Harvard architecture. Our experimental results
  demonstrate that a dynamically generated loop-unrolled SMC checksum runs
  approximately 90x faster than its non-SMC counterpart on x86-64 hardware,
  showing that well-designed SMC can simultaneously be smaller and faster while
  remaining infeasible to emulate without triggering measurable timing
  discrepancies.
keywords:
  - x86
  - x86-64
  - assembly language
  - self-modifying code
  - software protection
  - tamper proofing
  - protection mechanisms
